// NEGATIVE TEST -- a type that stores itself through a ZERO-LENGTH array is
// refused, and that refusal is a DECISION rather than an accident.
//
// This is the one place the recursive-layout analysis knowingly
// over-approximates. `[Z; 0]` stores no `Z`, so `Z` has a finite size and the
// cycle is broken with no `enum` on it -- the general rule ("an inhabited cycle
// contains an enum node") does not hold for this program. The analysis counts
// the edge anyway, because `labelled_occurrences` contributes an edge for `[T; N]`
// at every `N`.
//
// It is refused for two measured reasons, both named in the diagnostic:
//
//   * There is nothing to emit. Accepting it means emitting
//     `typedef struct Z { struct Z xs[0]; } Z;` -- an array of an INCOMPLETE
//     element type, which C has no spelling for. Measured with cc:
//     `error: array has incomplete element type 'struct Z'`. Accepting here only
//     moves the failure into gcc, which is the exact shape this analysis exists
//     to remove.
//   * There is nothing to construct. `[T; 0]` is uninhabited today -- the only
//     expression that could make one is the empty array literal, and the type
//     checker refuses it ("Empty array literals are not supported (cannot infer
//     type)", measured on `Holder { xs: [] }`).
//
// This fixture exists so the decision outlives the second reason. If the empty
// array literal ever becomes inferrable, the refusal is still correct on the
// first reason alone, and this row is where that is written down. Requirement
// N4-23 carries the same exclusion. (N4-22 is the POSITIVE row -- an enum
// payload naming its own enum -- and says nothing about arrays.)
struct Z {
    xs: [Z; 0],
}

fn main() {
    print("ok");
}
